Scope of ACE Reports
Each ACE report provides a structured audit of one or more related clinical research articles. Reports may examine randomized controlled trials, observational studies, meta-analyses, systematic reviews, clinical guidelines, or clusters of related publications.
Depending on the article(s) under review, an ACE audit may include checks for:

impossible or internally inconsistent summary statistics;
mismatches between reported test statistics, sample sizes, confidence intervals, and p values;
GRIM, GRIMMER, SPRITE, or related numerical consistency failures;
discrepancies between trial registrations and published outcomes;
duplicated, overlapping, or implausibly related datasets;
image or figure anomalies;
authorship, publication, or peer-review irregularities;
unexplained changes in sample size, analysis sets, or outcome reporting;
methodological errors that affect interpretation or downstream evidence synthesis.

When possible, ACE includes figures, tables, screenshots, code, and step-by-step explanations showing exactly where an error appears and how it was evaluated.

Evidence Standard
The goal of every ACE report is to separate what can be demonstrated from what can only be suspected. Every finding must satisfy three conditions:

the issue must be observable in the public record (reported data, text, images, metadata, or registrations);
the check must be reproducible by an independent reader;
the conclusion drawn must not exceed what the available evidence reasonably supports.

These conditions ensure that each finding is tied to a transparent evidentiary basis. ACE reports do not accuse any author, institution, or publisher of deliberate misconduct; they document what the record shows and let readers draw their own conclusions.

Suggesting a Study
If you come across a published study that looks problematic, you can write to ACE and suggest it. Readers, meta-analysts, editors, reviewers, and researchers who have noticed something in their own field are all welcome to do so.
If you would rather not be named, say so and ACE will not identify you publicly without your explicit permission. ACE will make all reasonable efforts to keep correspondence private, although privacy cannot be guaranteed against legal process.
Some concerns are not ACE’s to handle. If the matter appears criminal, or patients may be at risk, contact law enforcement or the relevant regulator. Formal misconduct allegations belong with the institution, funder, or research integrity body responsible for the work, which can compel access to data ACE cannot see. Writing to ACE is not a substitute for either.
Please do not send patient-identifiable data, or documents and datasets you are not permitted to share. What you send should rest on the published record and on material you are entitled to provide; ACE cannot accept or act on confidential internal documents.
A suggestion is most useful when it is specific. A general claim that a researcher, institution, journal, or public figure is “a fraud” is not something ACE can work from. What helps is naming the study, report, trial registration, dataset, figure, table, author group, journal, or publication record at issue and explaining what appears to be wrong. Useful things to include:

the article title, DOI, journal, or trial registration number;
the specific table, figure, outcome, image, statistic, or passage that appears problematic;
what appears to be wrong, such as impossible statistics, duplicated data, image manipulation, authorship irregularities, undisclosed protocol deviations, plagiarism, or paper-mill indicators;
any supporting documentation, screenshots, archived pages, PubPeer threads, registry records, code, calculations, or related publications;
a brief explanation of why the issue matters.

ACE reads everything it receives, but writing to ACE does not guarantee that a report will follow. Because capacity is limited, priority goes to concerns that are specific, tractable, reproducible, and relevant to the reliability of clinical evidence.
